<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Katsushika Hokusai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1760">1760</a>–<a href="/years/1849">1849</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Katsushika Hokusai was a Japanese painter and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukiyo-e">ukiyo-e</a> printmaker of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. His series Thirty-six Views of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-fuji">Mount Fuji</a>, published from about 1831 and including The Great Wave off Kanagawa, made the landscape print a major genre, and his sketchbooks, the Hokusai Manga, recorded everyday life in thousands of images. His work strongly influenced European art in the later nineteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/katsushika-hokusai.jpg" alt="Katsushika Hokusai" width="320" height="664" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: missing name · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hokusai_as_an_old_man.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
